 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "A medical researcher collects statistical data as follows:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>23% of senior citizens (65 or older) get the flu each year.</li>\n<li>32% of people under 65 get the flu each year.</li>\n<li>In the population, there are 15% senior citizens.</li>\n</ul>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 457991,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Are the events “being a senior” and “getting the flu” dependent or independent?\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "See Solution Manual \n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "These events are not independent since the probability of getting the flu is affected by age.\n\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 457992,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Find the probability that a person selected at random is a senior and will get the flu.\n\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "0.035"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Since we are interested in the probability that a person selected at random is a senior and will get the flu, use the probability formula: <br><span data-math=\"P\\left( {A\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;B} \\right) = P\\left( A \\right)\\cdot P\\left( {B\\;|\\;A} \\right),\\;\\mathrm{when}\\;A,\\;B\\;\\mathrm{are}\\;\\mathrm{dependent}\\;\\mathrm{events}\"></span><br>Note that the statement “23% of senior citizens (65 or older) get the flu each year” can be considered as a conditional probability where the probability that someone gets the flu, given that they are a senior citizen, is 0.23.<br><span data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}P{\\left(\\mathrm{senior}\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;\\mathrm{flu}\\right)}&amp;= P{\\left(\\mathrm{senior}\\right)}\\cdot P{\\left({\\mathrm{flu}\\;\\vert\\;\\mathrm{senior}}\\right)}\\\\P{\\left(\\mathrm{senior}\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;\\mathrm{flu}\\right)}&amp;=0.15\\cdot0.23=0.035\\end{aligned}\"></span>\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 457993,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Find the probability that a person selected at random is under 65 years old and will get the flu.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "0. 272 "
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Note that the statement “32% of people under 65 get the flu each year” can be considered as a conditional probability where the probability that someone gets the flu, given that they are under 65, is 0.32. Also note that since there are 15% senior citizens in the population, this implies that the remaining 85% of the population is under the age of 65.<br><span data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}P{\\left(\\mathrm{under}\\;65\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;\\mathrm{flu}\\right)}&amp;=P{\\left(\\mathrm{under}\\;65\\right)}\\cdot  P{\\left({\\mathrm{flu}\\;\\vert\\;\\mathrm{under}\\;65}\\right)}\\\\P{\\left(\\mathrm{under}\\;65\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;\\mathrm{flu}\\right)}&amp;=0.85\\cdot 0.32=0.272\\end{aligned}\"></span>\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
